  "response": "Between 1787 and 1788, New York newspapers carried a sustained series of essays urging ratification of the proposed Constitution. The Federalist Papers consist of 85 essays published under the pseudonym \"Publius.\" That shared signature hid a collaboration whose members were known to contemporaries, even if the assignment of individual essays was not: Alexander Hamilton, James Madison, and John Jay wrote the Federalist Papers. The three did not contribute equally. By contrast with the shared authorship of the series as a whole, John Jay wrote twenty of the Federalist essays, a share that has never been seriously contested. The difficulty that occupied historians for a century and a half lay elsewhere, in a block of twelve essays that either Hamilton or Madison could plausibly have written.\n\nThe dispute began with the authors' own testimony. Hamilton left a list of authorship attributions shortly before his 1804 duel with Aaron Burr, and Madison, at a later date, claimed authorship of essays that Hamilton's list had attributed to Hamilton. Neither witness could be dismissed, yet the two accounts conflicted, so at least one of the following must hold, perhaps both: Hamilton's authorship list contained errors about the disputed essays, or Madison's authorship claims about the disputed essays contained errors. Partisan loyalty and impressions of style could not break the deadlock, because each side could cite passages that sounded characteristic of its preferred author. By contrast, Frederick Mosteller and David Wallace conducted the landmark statistical study of the disputed papers, replacing contested memory with measurement.\n\nTheir method is frequently misdescribed. Although Mosteller and Wallace relied primarily on sentence length to attribute the disputed papers, they focused on the frequencies of common function words. These are the prepositions, conjunctions, and particles that writers use habitually and almost without thought. Because such words carry little topical content, their rates remain stable across subjects, and because they are used unconsciously, they resist deliberate imitation. One word served as an especially sharp discriminator; for example, Hamilton used the word \"upon\" far more often than Madison did. That observation is simply a restatement of a more basic fact: Madison used the word \"upon\" much less frequently than Hamilton did, so a disputed essay in which \"upon\" appears rarely or not at all looks far more Madisonian than Hamiltonian. A single word cannot decide a case by itself, but a collection of words with stable and divergent rates can, provided their evidence is combined in a principled way.\n\nThat combination required an explicit statistical framework, and here the study's design has been described in two incompatible ways. Either the Mosteller\u2013Wallace analysis used Bayesian inference, or the Mosteller\u2013Wallace analysis did not use Bayesian inference; the two descriptions cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. In the Bayesian reading, prior odds between the two candidates are updated by likelihoods drawn from models of word frequency, yielding posterior odds of authorship for each paper. The rarity of \"upon\" in the contested texts indicates the result that ended the controversy: Mosteller and Wallace concluded that Madison wrote all twelve disputed Federalist Papers. That conclusion settles the apparent tension in how their method has been described, since a verdict assigning every one of the twelve essays to Madison could rest only on discriminating word rates, which sentence length cannot supply. The conclusion was also what led to the Bayesian framing of the study, because a verdict that strong demanded posterior odds capable of expressing it for each paper. The result coincided with Madison's later claims, and in the decades since it has served as a standard demonstration of how quantitative stylometry can resolve a question that documentary testimony had left open.",
